mathrm{rand}}\\in [{r}_{i},2{r}_{i}] uniformly at random and let 𝒳 be the set of balls B\\in ℬ which intersect the sphere of radius {r}_{\\mathrm{rand}} centered at {p}_{i}.\n## 4 Further results\n\nWe start by giving a construction that shows that our main results are asymptotically optimal. We then generalize these results to a larger class of geometric objects.\n\n###### Proposition 4.1.\n\nFor each n,d\\ge 1 and n\\le m\\le \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}\\right), there exists an intersection graph of n balls or spheres in {ℝ}^{d} with at most m edges for which every balanced separator has size at least {\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{d}({m}^{1/d}{n}^{1-2/d}).\n\n### Proof.\n\nLet V={\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },k\\}}^{d} for k=\\lfloor {n}^{1/d}\\rfloor . Define G=(V,E) where x,y\\in V are adjacent if \\Vert x-y\\Vert \\le r. Choosing 1\\le r={\\mathrm{\\Theta }}_{d}({(m/n)}^{1/d}) appropriately, we can ensure that G has {\\mathrm{\\Theta }}_{d}(m) edges. Note that G is both the intersection graph of the radius r/2 balls centered at the points of V and the intersection graph of the radius r/2 spheres centered at the points of V. We will show that every balanced separator of G has size at least {\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{d}({m}^{1/d}{n}^{1-2/d}).\n\nWe first handle the case when r=1. Suppose that 𝒳 is a balanced separator with |𝒳|\\le {k}^{d}/3. Let A\\subset V be the union of some connected components of G\\setminus 𝒳, satisfying {k}^{d}/3\\le |A|\\le 2{k}^{d}/3. When r=1, the graph G is known as the grid graph on {\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },k\\}}^{d}. Then the isoperimetric inequality on the grid graph [2] implies that there are at least {k}^{d-1} edges between A and V\\setminus A. However, all such edges must have one endpoint in 𝒳. Since the maximum degree of G is 2d, we conclude that |𝒳|\\ge {k}^{d-1}/2d\\ge {\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{d}({n}^{1-1/d}), as desired.\n\nTo handle the general case, let {r}^{\\prime }=r/(2\\sqrt{d}). For simplicity, assume that {r}^{\\prime } is an integer that divides k, say k={r}^{\\prime }{k}^{\\prime }. Divide {\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },k\\}}^{d} into {({k}^{\\prime })}^{d} subcubes of side length {r}^{\\prime }. Note that every vertex in one subcube is adjacent in G to every vertex in an adjacent subcube. Similar to before, suppose that 𝒳 is a balanced separator with |𝒳|\\le {k}^{d}/12 and A is a union of connected components of G\\setminus 𝒳 with {k}^{d}/3\\le |A|\\le 2{k}^{d}/3. Write B=V\\setminus (𝒳\\cup A). We know |B|\\ge {k}^{d}/4. Define {A}^{\\prime }\\subseteq {\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },{k}^{\\prime }\\}}^{d} where a point lies in {A}^{\\prime } if the corresponding {r}^{\\prime }\\times \\mathrm{\\cdots }\\times {r}^{\\prime } subcube of {\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },k\\}}^{d} contains at least one point of A. Define {B}^{\\prime }\\subseteq {\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },{k}^{\\prime }\\}}^{d} similarly. First note that {A}^{\\prime } and {B}^{\\prime } are disjoint – if they shared a vertex then A,B would contain points in the same subcube which have distance at most \\sqrt{d}{r}^{\\prime }<r.\n\nWrite {X}^{\\prime }={\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },{k}^{\\prime }\\}}^{d}\\setminus ({A}^{\\prime }\\cup {B}^{\\prime }). Note that |{A}^{\\prime }|\\ge |A|/{({r}^{\\prime })}^{d}\\ge {({k}^{\\prime })}^{d}/3 and similarly |{B}^{\\prime }|\\ge |B|/{({r}^{\\prime })}^{d}\\ge {({k}^{\\prime })}^{d}/4. Thus |{A}^{\\prime }|\\le {({k}^{\\prime })}^{d}-|{B}^{\\prime }|\\le 3{({k}^{\\prime })}^{d}/4. Applying the isoperimetric inequality to the grid graph on {\\{1,\\mathrm{\\dots },{k}^{\\prime }\\}}^{d}, we conclude that there are at least {({k}^{\\prime })}^{d-1} edges between {A}^{\\prime } and {B}^{\\prime }\\cup {X}^{\\prime }. However, there are no edges between {A}^{\\prime },{B}^{\\prime } because any two points in two adjacent subcube are at distance at most 2\\sqrt{d}{r}^{\\prime }=r. Thus we conclude that |{X}^{\\prime }|\\ge {({k}^{\\prime })}^{d-1}/(2d). Note though that a point lies in {X}^{\\prime } only if the corresponding subcube lies entirely in 𝒳. We thus see that |𝒳|\\ge {({r}^{\\prime })}^{d}{({k}^{\\prime })}^{d-1}/(2d)={\\mathrm{\\Theta }}_{d}({m}^{1/d}{n}^{1-2/d}). ◀\n\nOur main results on ball and sphere separators, Theorems 2.2 and 3.1, hold for more general classes of geometric objects. We say that a set B\\subset {ℝ}^{d} is C-fat if B contains a ball of radius r and is contained inside a ball of radius Cr for some r. Our results hold for fat convex bodies and for the boundaries of fat convex bodies. (See [11, 4] for related results under slightly different hypotheses.)\n\n## 5 Algorithmic implementations\n\nSuppose we are given ℬ as a collection of centers and radii of balls. We give a randomized linear time algorithm that computes a small separator for G(ℬ).\n\n###### Theorem 5.1.\n\nThe separator for intersection graphs of balls given in Theorem 2.2 can be computed by a randomized linear time algorithm that succeeds with probability at least 1/2.\n\n### Proof.\n\nWrite 𝒫 for the centers of the balls of ℬ. Let r be the smallest radius such that there exists a ball {B}_{0} of radius r that contains at least {9}^{-d}n points of 𝒫. Fix such a ball {B}_{0}. Then for any p\\in 𝒫\\cap {B}_{0}, the ball of radius 2r centered at p contains {B}_{0} and thus contains at least {9}^{-d}n points of 𝒫.\n\nOur first goal is to efficiently find an approximation for this ball {B}_{0}. Fix a large constant {C}_{d} and sample {C}_{d}\\cdot {9}^{d} points {p}_{1},{p}_{2},\\mathrm{\\dots }\\in 𝒫 uniformly at random. For each point {p}_{i}, compute the smallest {r}_{i} such that the ball of radius {r}_{i} centered at {p}_{i} contains {9}^{-d}n points of 𝒫. This can be computed in linear time for each {p}_{i}. Say we succeed if {\\mathrm{min}}_{i}{r}_{i}\\le 2r. By the discussion above, we succeed if some {p}_{i}\\in 𝒫\\cap {B}_{0}, which occurs with probability at least\n\n | 1-{\\left(1-{9}^{-d}\\right)}^{{C}_{d}\\cdot {9}^{d}}\\ge 1-{e}^{-{C}_{d}}.  | \n\nPick 1\\le i\\le {C}_{d}\\cdot {9}^{d} such that {r}_{i} is minimal. As in the proof of Theorem 2.2, pick {r}_{\\mathrm{rand}}\\in [{r}_{i},2{r}_{i}] uniformly at random and let 𝒳 be the set of balls B\\in ℬ which intersect the sphere of radius {r}_{\\mathrm{rand}} centered at {p}_{i}. This set can be computed in linear time. Now we still have\n\n | 𝔼[|𝒳|]\\le 4{\\left(\\sum _{B\\in ℬ}p{(B)}^{{\\scriptscriptstyle \\frac{1}{d-1}}}\\right)}^{1-{\\scriptscriptstyle \\frac{1}{d}}},  | \n\nso by Markov’s inequality,\n\n | \\mathrm{Pr}\\left[|𝒳|\\le 4{C}_{d}{\\left(\\sum _{B\\in ℬ}p{(B)}^{{\\scriptscriptstyle \\frac{1}{d-1}}}\\right)}^{1-{\\scriptscriptstyle \\frac{1}{d}}}\\right]\\ge 1-{C}_{d}^{-1}.  | \n\nNow the set 𝒳 is a (1-{9}^{-d})-balanced separator for G(ℬ). To see this, note that the ball of radius {r}_{i} centered at {p}_{i} contains {B}_{0} which contains \\lceil {9}^{-d}n\\rceil points of 𝒫. Furthermore, the ball of radius 2{r}_{i} centered at {p}_{i} can be covered by at most {8}^{d} translates of {B}_{0}, so it contains at most {8}^{d}\\lceil {9}^{-d}\\rceil n<(1-{9}^{-d})n points of 𝒫.\n\nThus we have a randomized linear time algorithm which computes a small (1-{9}^{-d})-balanced separator for G(ℬ) with probability at least 1-{C}_{d}^{-1}. By Lemma 2.5, for {C}_{d} sufficiently large, we can iterate this algorithm \\lceil {\\mathrm{log}}_{1-{9}^{-d}}(2/3)\\rceil times to produce a \\frac{2}{3}-balanced separator for G(ℬ) still in linear time with success probability at least \\frac{1}{2}. ◀\n\nSuppose we are given 𝒮 as a list of centers and radii of the spheres. In quadratic time we can compute which pairs of spheres intersect and which pairs are contained in each other. From this we can run the algorithm described in Theorem 3.1 in quadratic time. The only potential difficulty is in selecting the minimal radius ball {B}_{\\mathrm{in}}, but this can be handled the same way is in Theorem 5.1.\n\n###### Theorem 5.2.\n\nThe separator for intersection graphs of spheres given in Theorem 3.1 can